          <rect class="box-bk" x="6" y="338" width="164" height="110" />
          <svg x="14" y={393 - screenArt.h * 74 / screenArt.w} width="148" height={screenArt.h * 148 / screenArt.w} viewBox={`0 0 ${screenArt.w} ${screenArt.h}`} shape-rendering="crispEdges">{screenArt.paths.map(p => <path fill={p.fill} d={p.d} />)}</svg>
        </g>
        <rect class="box" x="182" y="338" width="172" height="50" /><text x="268" y="360" text-anchor="middle">Video BIOS</text><text class="t-sm" x="268" y="379" text-anchor="middle">the PC's own code</text>
        <g data-s="4"><path class="wire-hi" d="M180 50 V92" /><path class="wire arrow dash" d="M268 280 V330" /><text class="t-cy" x="190" y="414">not 36h:</text><text class="t-cy" x="190" y="434">passed on</text></g>
      </svg>
    </Steps>
    </div>

    <div class="ab-call">
      <h3 class="ab-wide">One call, in 1993 and today</h3>
      <Window title="CALL.ASM  his API" kind="editor" class="ab-code" data-pairs>
        <ol class="ab-lines">{CALL.map(c => <li data-p={c.p}><code>{c.a[0]}</code><span>{c.a[1]}</span></li>)}</ol>
      </Window>
      <Window title="The same call as a local web API" kind="editor" class="ab-code" data-pairs>
        <ol class="ab-lines ab-lines--js">{WEB.map(c => <li data-p={c.p}><code>{c.l}</code></li>)}</ol>
      </Window>
      <p class="ab-wide ab-note">Point at a line to see its partner. The string is plain ASCII: <code>~gtk ~guvfd</code> is <span lang="ar">الفن العربي</span>, typed on an English keyboard as if it had Arabic key caps.</p>
    </div>

    <ThenNow
      title="Then and now"
      rows={[
        { then: 'A TSR: it ends, but stays in memory', now: 'A background service, always running', why: 'Started once, then there for every program.' },
        { then: 'Its own function number on the video service, INT 10h', now: 'A local API endpoint', why: 'One address every program already knows.' },
        { then: 'A second number: 7 draws, 5 measures, 3 picks a font', now: 'The routes of that API', why: 'A number picks the function.' },
        { then: 'Inputs in four of the CPU’s own variables', now: 'The request’s parameters', why: 'The cheapest way to hand values to code you never linked with.' },
        { then: '“This utility is already active in your RAM.”', now: 'A single-instance lock', why: 'Run it twice and it politely refuses to load again.' },
      ]}
    />
    <Deep title="For engineers: the hook and the 11 functions">
      <Listing
        title="ARABART.EXE · install (CS:0000) and the handler (CS:003C)"
        lines={[
          { off: '0000', code: 'mov  ax,3510h', note: 'ask DOS: who answers INT 10h now?' },
          { off: '0003', code: 'int  21h' },
          { off: '0007', code: 'mov  si,003Ch', note: 'my own handler' },
          { off: '000C', code: 'mov  cx,12' },
          { off: '0010', code: 'repe cmpsb', note: 'the same 12 bytes, my name tag DALYASSER? then I am already here', hi: true },
          { off: '0027', code: 'mov  [0051h],es', note: 'keep the old address inside my own jump' },
          { off: '002B', code: 'mov  [004Fh],bx' },
          { off: '002F', code: 'mov  ax,2510h / int 21h', note: 'INT 10h now comes to me first' },
          { off: '0048', code: 'sti', note: 'every INT 10h call lands here, just after the name tag' },
          { off: '0049', code: 'cmp  ah,36h', note: 'is it for ARABART?', hi: true },
          { off: '004C', code: 'je   0053h', note: 'yes: AL picks one of 11 functions' },
          { off: '004E', code: 'jmp  far old_int10', note: 'no: on to the video BIOS, untouched' },
          { off: '74FC', code: 'int  27h', note: 'end, and keep 30,110 bytes resident' },
        ]}
        foot="Offsets are inside his unpacked program. The name tag DALYASSER sits at 003F, right after the first jump."
      />
      <div class="ab-api">
        <table>
          <caption>INT 10h, AH = 36h. AL picks the function</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">AL</th><th scope="col">Function</th><th scope="col">Registers</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{API.map(([al, f, r]) => <tr><td>{al}h</td><td>{f}</td><td>{r}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Style keys inside the text switch styles as it draws: <code>@</code> bold, <code>!</code> italic, <code>#</code> underline, <code>$</code> double size, <code>*</code> all off. So a whole formatted line is one ordinary C string, like markup.</p>
    </Deep>
  </section>

  <section class="ab-sec" id="joining" aria-labelledby="h-joining">
    <div class="ab-text">
      <h2 id="h-joining">Two bits choose every shape</h2>
      <p class="ab-lead">An Arabic letter changes shape with its neighbors. ARABART picks the right shape for every letter, as you type.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="ab-forms" aria-label="The letter ع in its four shapes, drawn by his engine">
      {forms.map(f => <li><Pix img={f.img} scale={3} /><span>{f.form}</span></li>)}
    </ol>
    <p class="ab-p">One letter, four shapes: on its own, at the start of a word, in the middle, at the end. Pull a letter out of the word below and watch its neighbors change. Every letter here is drawn by his engine from his own bytes, not by a modern font.</p>
    <DragWord />

    <h3 class="ab-h3">The keyboard is the alphabet</h3>
    <div class="ab-cols">
      <p>In 1993 a PC had no Unicode. So Yasser stored Arabic as the English keys a typist presses on an Arabic keyboard: <kbd>g</kbd> is <span lang="ar">ل</span>, <kbd>f</kbd> is <span lang="ar">ب</span>, <kbd>h</kbd> is <span lang="ar">ا</span>. A whole line of Arabic fits in a normal C string, in any editor. This is the <Term id="key-encoding">Arabic-keyboard encoding</Term>.</p>
      <p>A 96-byte table turns each key into one byte. Think of it as a JavaScript object from key to number. And that number already knows how its letter joins. Its top bit says the letter can reach left, to the next letter. The bit below it says the letter can reach right, to the one before. Pick a key, then flip its bits.</p>
    </div>
    <LetterByte />
    <p class="ab-p">The two bits are the shape: <code>00</code> isolated, <code>01</code> final, <code>10</code> initial, <code>11</code> medial. So the byte is the shape, and it is also the glyph’s number in the font. No lookup, no search: the code goes straight to the picture. The table stores every letter with all the hands it can ever use, and joining only ever takes hands away.</p>

    <h3 class="ab-h3">His loop, line by line</h3>
    <div class="ab-cols">
      <p>One pass over the text does all the joining. For each letter and the next: if this letter reaches left and the next one reaches right, they stay joined. If not, both let go. In JavaScript the rule is one line:</p>
      <pre class="ab-js"><code>if (!(code & 0x80 && next & 0x40)) {'{'} code &= 0x7f; next &= 0xbf; {'}'}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <ShapeLoop />
    <div class="ab-cols ab-after">
      <p>Style keys never break a word. The keys <code>@</code> bold, <code>!</code> italic, <code>#</code> underline, <code>$</code> double size and <code>*</code> reset all become codes he took from the middle shapes of the digits. Digits never join, so no letter can ever turn into a style key, and the loop simply steps over them. Run it on <span lang="ar">الفن</span> with <code>@</code> to watch a word stay joined across a style change.</p>
      <p>Lam-alef, <span lang="ar">لا</span>, has its own key, <kbd>b</kbd>, just as on the Arabic keyboard. And the joining loop that shapes your letters at the top of this page is byte for byte the one inside PESM, his program from 1991.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ab-sec" id="small" aria-labelledby="h-small">
    <div class="ab-pair">
    <div class="ab-text">
      <h2 id="h-small" class="ab-big">1,448 bytes</h2>
      <p class="ab-lead">That is all of ARABART’s code: 11 functions, the joining, the measuring and every style. Most of the rest is his five fonts.</p>
      <ul class="ab-facts">
        <li>Drawing <span lang="ar">الفن العربي ٤,١٥</span>, 16 keys in font 3, takes his code <strong>25,193 instructions</strong> for 1,357 pixels: 18.6 instructions a pixel.</li>
        <li>Double size draws <strong>4 times the pixels</strong> for only 42% more work.</li>
        <li>Joining the four letters of his own name, <span lang="ar">ياسر</span>, takes <strong>170 instructions</strong>.</li>
        <li>It talks to the graphics card <strong>424 times</strong> for those 1,357 pixels, about 3 pixels a write.</li>
        <li>It uses only <strong>8086 instructions</strong>, so it runs on the processor of the first IBM PC.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <SizeBars
      title="BYTES, to scale"
      items={[
        { label: 'His code, all 11 functions', bytes: 1448, his: true },
        { label: 'His keyboard table', bytes: 96, his: true },
        { label: 'His five fonts', bytes: 27932, his: true },
        { label: 'All of ARABART in memory', bytes: 30110, his: true },
        { label: 'ARABART.EXE on disk, packed', bytes: 11183, his: true },
        { label: 'The screenshot of his demo on this page', bytes: 36295 },
        { label: 'gsap.min.js, the animation library this page uses', bytes: 72435 },
      ]}
    />
    </div>
    <div class="ab-text ab-wide-text">
      <h3>One write, up to eight pixels</h3>
      <p>The slow way to draw is to ask the PC’s built-in video code for one pixel at a time. ARABART skips that and talks to the graphics card itself. It tells the card the color once, in its <Term id="set-reset">Set/Reset</Term> setting. It marks which of eight pixels to paint, in the <Term id="bit-mask">Bit Mask</Term>. Then one write paints them all. In GPU terms, it is a fill through a stencil. When it is done, it puts the card’s settings back, so Borland’s graphics keep working on the same screen.</p>
    </div>
    <Steps
      title="One byte of the screen, one write"
      steps={[
        'Tell the card the color once: <code>Set/Reset = 14</code>, yellow. All four of the card’s color layers take it.',
        'Say which pixels of this byte to paint: <code>Bit Mask = 0011 1100</code>, the four in the middle.',
        'Read the byte first. The card keeps a copy of it in a hidden buffer, so the pixels you do not paint keep their color.',
        'Write. The card paints just the four chosen pixels, in every color layer at once: one write, up to eight pixels.',
      ]}
    >
      <svg class="dg" viewBox="0 0 640 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-vga-t">
        <title id="ab-vga-t">The Set/Reset register holds yellow, the Bit Mask picks four of eight pixels, a read fills the latches, and one write paints the four pixels.</title>
        <g data-s="1+"><rect class="box-hi" x="10" y="10" width="196" height="54" /><text class="t-bk" x="108" y="33" text-anchor="middle">Set/Reset = 14</text><text class="t-bk" x="108" y="53" text-anchor="middle">the color, once</text></g>
        <g data-s="2+"><rect class="box-hi" x="222" y="10" width="196" height="54" /><text class="t-bk" x="320" y="33" text-anchor="middle">Bit Mask = 3Ch</text><text class="t-bk" x="320" y="53" text-anchor="middle">which pixels</text></g>
        <g data-s="3+"><rect class="box" x="434" y="10" width="196" height="54" /><text x="532" y="33" text-anchor="middle">Latches</text><text class="t-sm" x="532" y="53" text-anchor="middle">the old byte, 4 planes</text></g>
        {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7].map(i => <text class="t-cy" x={128 + i * 56} y="102" text-anchor="middle" data-s="2+">{[0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0][i]}</text>)}
        <rect x="112" y="122" width="448" height="56" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" class="t-sm" opacity="0.35" transform="translate(9 -9)" />
        <rect x="112" y="122" width="448" height="56" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" class="t-sm" opacity="0.5" transform="translate(6 -6)" />
        <rect x="112" y="122" width="448" height="56" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" class="t-sm" opacity="0.7" transform="translate(3 -3)" />
        {[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7].map(i => <rect class="box" x={102 + i * 56} y="128" width="52" height="52" />)}
        {[2, 3, 4, 5].map(i => <rect class="box-hi" x={102 + i * 56} y="128" width="52" height="52" data-s="4" />)}
        <text class="t-sm" x="102" y="206">one byte of the screen: 8 pixels, 4 color planes behind it</text>
        <path class="wire-hi arrow" data-s="3" d="M560 140 C600 130 600 90 560 70" />
        <g data-s="4"><text class="t-yl" x="102" y="240">One write: 4 pixels, 4 planes.</text><text class="t-sm" x="102" y="262">The other pixels come back from the latches unchanged.</text></g>
      </svg>
      <svg slot="narrow" class="dg" viewBox="0 0 360 322" role="img" aria-labelledby="ab-vga-tn">
        <title id="ab-vga-tn">Set/Reset holds the color, the Bit Mask picks pixels, a read fills the latches, one write paints them.</title>
        <g data-s="1+"><rect class="box-hi" x="6" y="6" width="348" height="44" /><text class="t-bk" x="180" y="34" text-anchor="middle">Set/Reset = 14: the color, once</text></g>
        <g data-s="2+"><rect class="box-hi" x="6" y="58" width="348" height="44" /><text class="t-bk" x="180" y="86" text-anchor="middle">Bit Mask = 3Ch: which pixels</text></g>
        <g data-s="3+"><rect class="box" x="6" y="110" width="348" height="44" /><text x="180" y="138" text-anchor="middle">Latches: the old byte</text></g>
