      </svg>
    </Steps>
    <Deep title="For engineers: the two places it talks to the card">
      <Listing
        title="ARABART.EXE · the VGA Graphics Controller"
        lines={[
          { off: '02F9', code: 'mov  dx,3CEh', note: 'the Graphics Controller' },
          { off: '02FC', code: 'mov  al,1 / out dx,al', note: 'register 1: Enable Set/Reset' },
          { off: '02FF', code: 'inc  dx / mov al,0Fh / out dx,al', note: 'all 4 planes take their color from Set/Reset' },
          { off: '0307', code: 'inc  dx / mov al,bl / out dx,al', note: 'register 0, Set/Reset: the color', hi: true },
          { off: '0506', code: 'mov  ah,8', note: 'register 8, the Bit Mask' },
          { off: '050B', code: 'xchg al,ah / out dx,ax', note: 'index and value in one 16-bit OUT' },
          { off: '050E', code: 'mov  ah,es:[di]', note: 'read: fills the latches' },
          { off: '0511', code: 'mov  es:[di],al', note: 'write: only the masked pixels change', hi: true },
        ]}
        foot="At the end of every call (055D) he sets Enable Set/Reset back to 0 and the Bit Mask to FFh, so Borland’s BGI graphics keep working on the same screen."
      />
    </Deep>
  </section>

  <section class="ab-sec" id="fonts" aria-labelledby="h-fonts">
    <div class="ab-text">
      <h2 id="h-fonts">Five fonts, drawn one run at a time</h2>
      <p class="ab-lead">Yasser drew every letter of five fonts himself: 27,932 bytes of them. He stored each letter not as a grid of pixels but as runs, like pen strokes: skip this many pixels, paint that many.</p>
    </div>
    <GlyphRuns />
    <div class="ab-cols ab-after">
      <p>The drawing loop only ever visits strokes, never an empty pixel. And because a letter is a list of strokes, every style below is a small change to each stroke. Each font starts with a table of 256 glyph addresses, like the index at the front of a book. Slots 0 to 2 can never hold a letter. So he kept the font’s own sizes there: slot 1 holds the line height, slot 2 the depth of the 3-D shadow. Letters shared between fonts simply point to the same bytes.</p>
      <p>Font 5 has just six glyphs of its own, 50 pixels tall, for the letters of <span lang="ar">الشمس</span>, The Sun. He made them for the title of The Brave Sun. Every other slot points back into font 1. Pick font 5 above and watch the sun’s letters paint themselves.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ab-sec" id="effects" aria-labelledby="h-effects">
    <div class="ab-text">
      <h2 id="h-effects">Bold, italic, 3-D: one loop draws them all</h2>
      <p class="ab-lead">Every style is a small change to a run, so one drawing loop handles all of them, and most cost almost nothing. Every number below is a count taken from his own program as it draws.</p>
    </div>
    <StyleLab />
    <h3 class="ab-h3">Why the italic stays joined</h3>
    <div class="ab-cols">
      <p>His italic moves each row one pixel left of the row below. The clever part is where the slant starts: at the line, the same for every letter. The strokes that join the letters sit on that line, so the slant never pulls them apart.</p>
      <p>Measure the slant from the top of each letter instead, and a tall <span lang="ar">ا</span> slides much further than a short <span lang="ar">ع</span>. The word breaks. Run both below.</p>
    </div>
    <ItalicJoin />
  </section>

  <section class="ab-sec" id="programs" aria-labelledby="h-programs">
    <div class="ab-text">
      <h2 id="h-programs">Where it went to work</h2>
      <p class="ab-lead">His own programs called ARABART for every word of Arabic they show. Here are three of them, and each one runs in your browser.</p>
    </div>
    <Programs items={PROGRAMS} />
  </section>

  <section class="ab-sec" id="path" aria-labelledby="h-path">
    <div class="ab-text">
      <h2 id="h-path">From PESM to 7.15</h2>
      <p class="ab-lead">ARABART grew from 1991 to 1993, version by version. Each step made it easier for the next program to write Arabic.</p>
    </div>
    <Steps
      title="Three versions of one engine"
      steps={[
        '<b>1991, PESM.</b> The engine lives inside one program. PESM draws the Basmallah 16 times in different colors, sizes and styles, and it carries its own copy of the joining code and the glyphs. Like copying a library’s source into your project.',
        '<b>1993, ARABART 4.15.</b> The engine moves into its own program that stays in memory: five fonts inside, 30,110 bytes in all. DEMO, EDITOR and The Brave Sun all call the same copy. Like a shared service every app on the machine uses.',
        '<b>1993, ARAB-ART 7.15.</b> Made for The Brave Sun: the fonts move out into their own file, <code>ARAB-ART.FNT</code>, 20,264 bytes that begin with his signature, <code>YASSER DOT DESIGN</code>. Like loading a web font file instead of building the font into the page.',
      ]}
